'''Empresa de Pastissos i Pa, S.A.''' (EPP) is a Balboan {{w|food processing}} company and the country's largest {{w|wheat}} importer and {{w|flour}} miller. Headquartered in [[Portnou]], the canal terminus city in [[Estret Province|Estret]], the company imports wheat from [[Burawa]] and [[Louyang]], mills it into flour, and produces bread, pastries, and pasta for the domestic market. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker EPP.

EPP is a vertically integrated grain company — controlling the supply chain from wheat import through flour milling to finished baked goods and pasta. The company benefits from the structural overvaluation of the [[International Dollar]] (ID), which makes wheat imports cheap on the input side, giving EPP a cost advantage over potential importers of finished baked goods. The company is primarily domestic-market-focused and is one of the largest food companies in Balboa by revenue.

== Operations ==

=== Wheat importing ===

EPP is Balboa's largest wheat importer, handling approximately 600,000 of the 800,000–1,000,000 tonnes of wheat imported annually. Wheat is sourced primarily from [[Burawa]] and [[Louyang]] and shipped through the [[Portnou|port of Portnou]], where EPP maintains a dedicated grain terminal with storage silos and handling facilities. The company's port-side location minimises transport costs from ship to mill.

The overvalued ID is central to EPP's business model: wheat is purchased in international markets at prices set in weaker currencies, and the strong ID reduces the cost of imports. This input-side advantage — the same structural feature that underpins Balboa's petroleum refining and assembly sectors — allows EPP to mill flour and produce baked goods at costs competitive with imported finished products.

=== Flour milling ===

EPP operates two flour mills:

* Molí del Port (Portnou): The company's primary milling facility, located adjacent to the grain terminal in [[Portnou]]. With a milling capacity of approximately 400,000 tonnes of wheat per year, it is the largest flour mill in Balboa. The mill produces a range of flour grades for bakery, pastry, and pasta production, as well as industrial flour for external sale to smaller bakeries and food manufacturers.
* Molí de Sant Bart (Sant Bart, Jala): The original mill, established in the 1940s when the company was still based in the interior. Modernised in the 1990s, it has a milling capacity of approximately 200,000 tonnes per year and serves the interior provinces, reducing transport costs from the coast.

Combined, EPP mills approximately 600,000 tonnes of wheat per year, yielding approximately 420,000 tonnes of flour (at a typical milling yield of 70 per cent). The remaining 180,000 tonnes of wheat bran and millfeed is sold to the livestock sector as animal feed.

=== Bakery and pastry ===

EPP's bakery division is the company's original business and its largest by revenue. The division operates production bakeries in Portnou, Sant Bart, and [[Sant Llàtzer]] (Migjorn), producing:

* Bread: Sliced bread, rolls, and traditional Balboan breads under the Pa Nostra brand. EPP is the largest commercial bread producer in Balboa, supplying supermarkets, convenience stores (including [[Doble8]]), and food service customers.
* Pastries: Cakes, croissants, pastries, and sweet baked goods under the Dolços de l'Istme brand. The pastry division operates both industrial-scale production for retail and a smaller artisanal line for premium customers.

The bakery and pastry division accounts for approximately 55 per cent of EPP's revenue.

=== Pasta ===

EPP's pasta and noodle division, established in the 1990s, produces dried pasta — spaghetti, macaroni, penne, and other shapes — under the Pasta de l'Istme brand, and instant and fresh noodles under the Fideus de l'Istme brand. The division was created to capture a share of the growing domestic market for pasta and noodles, which had previously been served by imports from [[Volisania]], [[Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]. The overvalued ID makes imported pasta and noodles expensive relative to domestically produced products, giving EPP a natural cost advantage.

The division's most successful product is Pebbre, a line of instant cup noodles modelled after the [[Nakamian Balboan people|Nakamian]]-style instant noodles popularised by the [[Nakamizu]] commercial network. Pebbre cups are sold in convenience stores across Balboa, including [[Doble8]], and have become one of the most recognisable packaged food brands in the country. The product's name — a stylisation of "pebre", meaning "pepper" in Volisanian — reflects its seasoning profile, which blends Zong-style noodle conventions with Balboan and Aethiopian spice traditions.

The pasta and noodle division accounts for approximately 25 per cent of EPP's revenue. Production facilities are co-located with the Molí del Port in Portnou, using flour milled on-site.

=== Industrial flour ===

EPP sells approximately 150,000 tonnes of flour per year to external customers — smaller bakeries, food manufacturers, and institutional buyers — under the Farina d'Or brand. The industrial flour business accounts for approximately 20 per cent of EPP's revenue and reinforces the company's position as the dominant grain processor in Balboa.

== History ==

EPP was founded in 1956 in [[Sant Bart]] by [[Jordi Ferrer i Soldevila]], a grain merchant who had previously traded in rice and maize in the interior. The company was established during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]], when the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]] — which controlled the interior but not the canal zone — was dependent on wheat imports routed through the AC-administered port of [[Portnou]]. President Ríos's economic liberalisation programme (1956–1959), which relaxed restrictions on private commercial enterprise, created the conditions for a private grain import company to operate.

The company's original business was wheat importing: Ferrer sourced wheat from [[Burawa]] and [[Louyang]] and arranged its shipment through the AC-controlled canal zone to the interior. The Molí de Sant Bart was established in the early 1960s to mill the imported wheat into flour, beginning the company's vertical integration from grain import to flour processing.

After the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) returned the canal zone to Balboan sovereignty and completed reunification, EPP relocated its headquarters to [[Portnou]] to be closer to the port where wheat was imported. The Molí del Port was constructed in the late 1970s, and the company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1984. The bakery and pastry division was expanded in the 1980s through acquisitions of smaller bakeries, and the pasta and noodle division was established in 1993. The Molí de Sant Bart was modernised in 1996.

The Ferrer family remains the largest shareholder, with current chairman [[Marc Ferrer i Vidal]] representing the third generation of family ownership.

== Competitive position ==

EPP's competitive position is defined by the overvalued ID. The strong ID makes wheat imports from Burawa and Louyang cheap, giving EPP a cost advantage on the input side. Domestically milled flour and locally produced bread and pasta are cheaper than imported finished baked goods, which would be priced in the strong ID at the full retail margin. The [[BTPMEU]] common external tariff provides some protection from non-BTPMEU processed food imports, though the company's primary advantage is the input-side cost benefit rather than tariff protection.

EPP is not a significant exporter. The overvalued ID makes Balboan baked goods and pasta expensive on world markets, and the domestic market of 35 million consumers is large enough to absorb the company's production. The [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] is not a material factor for EPP, as the company's export volumes are negligible.

